    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to have a distribution of indistinguishable books (six identical books) such that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out.\n\nWe can use the principle of complementary counting to solve this problem. First, calculate the total number of possible distributions without any restrictions, and then subtract the two trivial cases where all books are in the library or all books are checked out. Finally, we will divide by 2 because the order in which the books are in the library does not matter (every arrangement that has the same number of books in the library is counted just once due to the indistinguishability).\n\n1. **Total number of possible distributions**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total different ways} = 2^6 = 64\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of ways where all books are in the library**:\n   This means \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 0 \\) (where \\( x \\) books are in the library and \\( y \\) books are checked out):\n   \\[\n   \\text{One book is always in the library} (6 ways)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Number of ways where all books are checked out**:\n   This means \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( y = 6 \\) (where \\( x \\) books are in the library and \\( y \\) books are checked out):\n   \\[\n   \\text{One book is checked out} (6 ways)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Subtract these cases from the total**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{One book in the library and no books checked out (trivial exclusion)}  - 2 = 64 - 6 = 58\n   \\]\n\nThus, there are 58 ways in which some books from six copies can be in the library and some checked out, under the conditions that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out.",
